<newsitem itemid="494611" id="root" date="1997-04-08" xml:lang="en">
<title>USA: Santa Barbara Bancorp Q1 net, shr rise.</title>
<headline>Santa Barbara Bancorp Q1 net, shr rise.</headline>
<dateline>SANTA BARBARA, Calif. 1997-04-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>($ in millions except per-share earnings):</p>
<p>						 Qtr Ended	Qtr Ended</p>
<p>						  3/31/97	   3/31/96</p>
<p> Interest income				  31.5	     24.0</p>
<p> Interest expense				(9.7)	    (9.1)</p>
<p> Net interest income			    21.8	     14.9</p>
<p> Provision for loan loss		     (4.7)	    (3.2)</p>
<p> Non-interest income			     6.9		5.1</p>
<p> Non-interest expense			 (14.0)	   (11.3)</p>
<p> Pre-tax income				   10.0		5.5</p>
<p> Taxes					     (3.4)	    (1.6)</p>
<p> Net income					  6.6		3.9</p>
<p> Earnings per share			     0.88	     0.51</p>
<p>						    As of	     As of</p>
<p>						   3/31/97	  3/31/96</p>
<p> Loans (including RALs)			719.9	    556.5</p>
<p> Allowance for Loan Loss			23.1	     15.2</p>
<p> Assets					  1,343.8	  1,191.3</p>
<p> Deposits					1,140.7	  1,027.2</p>
<p> Capital					   111.0	    102.3</p>
<p>NOTES: Company said increased profitability in current Q1 was due primarily to expanded tax Refund Anticipation Loan and Refund Transfer program which contributed $2.55 million after tax compared with $825,000 in the same quarter 1996.</p>
<p>There was also an increase of $2.8 million in interest from loans and leases over 1996 as loans other than RALs grew 28 percent from $553 million at the end of Q1 1996 to $710 million at March 31, 1997. About $57 million of this increase came from the leasing portfolio purchased last December.</p>
<p>-- New York Newsdesk 212 859-1610</p>
</text>
